  "query_preview": "..While the area will mostly be between weather systems \ntoday, there will be enough lingering moisture for limited/isolated \nshower activity (10 to 20 percent chances). Most locales will stay \ndry but can't rule out a passing shower anytime from early this \nmorning through the day with light rainfall amounts. As the next \nweather system approaches tonight, precipitation chances start to \npick up after 5 PM and continue through the evening hours, \nespecially from the Tucson area westward as showers become more \nwidespread with a slight chance of thunderstorms. \n\nOvernight tonight through Sunday and Sunday night, a potent weather \nsystem riding the subtropical jet will move through the region as a \nwest coast upper level trof moves inland. A strong atmospheric river \n(AR), level 3 on a AR 1-5 scale, will bring near record to record \nlevels of PWAT of (1.25\" to 1.50\") for November. As this AR moves \nover the area, expect widespread moderate to localized heavy rain \nand isolated embedded thunderstorms on Sunday. The timing for the \nmost widespread precipitation will initially be over the western \ndeserts during the morning, shifting east into the Tucson area late \nmorning through the afternoon, and then points east of Tucson during \nthe afternoon and continuing into the evening. Most locales will see \na 4-6 hour period of moderate to at times heavy rainfall with a few \nembedded thunderstorms. Upslope flow into the mountains will also \nenhance rainfall rates/totals for these locales. Additionally, given \nthe 30-40 kt 850-700mb southwest flow, expect breezy conditions with \ngusty winds over 40 mph especially within any convective lines \ncapable of bringing some of those stronger winds aloft to the \nsurface. While the most widespread heavier precipitation will end \nSunday night into Monday morning as the AR moves east of the region, \nPoPs still remain elevated as the upper trough moves across the \nnorthern half of Arizona. Most of the area is under slight risk from \nWPC for excessive rainfall Sunday with a very small snippet of far \nnorthern Graham, Pinal and Greenlee counties under moderate risk. We \nare looking at rainfall records for November 1st falling across much \nof the area. A Flood Watch is in effect for all of SE AZ from 11 pm \ntonight to 5 am Monday. While confidence is increasing in rainfall \ntotals, there is still some spread in the guidance. Below is a look \nat the expected totals, high and low end totals.\n\n-Expected: Valleys 0.50\"-1.75\", Mountains 1\"-3+\"   \n\n-Low end (9 in 10 chance):\n Valleys 0.20\"-1.25\", Mountains 0.50\"-1.5\" \n  \n-High end (1 in 10 chance): \n Valleys 1.50\"-3.00\", Mountains 2\"-4\" locally higher\n\nWith the expected rain on Sunday into early Monday we will see \na response in the area rivers, creeks and washes across SE AZ. \nSee hydrology section below for further details.\n\nThereafter, we'll get into a quieter weather regime early next week. \nAny lingering precipitation should come to an end by Monday \nafternoon for most locales with shortwave ridging and dry conditions \nTuesday and Wednesday. Thursday still looks dry as well as the flow \nbecomes southwest ahead of the next weather system. Ensembles are \nshowing more troughing Friday and Saturday but still some \nuncertainty on the specifics. Nonetheless, it's not looking too \nimpactful but 10 to 30 percent PoPs return Friday and Saturday. \n\nDaytime temperatures will mostly be below normal through the \nforecast period, with the coolest highs Sunday and Monday.",
